{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact d68e4280a9c7f4e6577d754f8f0c04476eed9d89, tree 346b680394075609a7af6a2fbe1551a4c2a822e0. All findings follow.\n\nF1 — P1 — The fixed-endpoint rule rejects its own creation-result pattern. Section 2 requires an endpoint slot to be fixed and permits setting it from an applied creation result, citing Grow site.ts:25. But a fixed native slot can be set only by the entry that opens its item; a result clause runs in a later entry. The cited Grow room.scope slot is deliberately fixed:false. In a separate scratch probe I validated the actual Grow site successfully, changed only room.refs.scope.fixed to true, and the unchanged native validator refused code fixed at acts.create-room.sends.0.create.result.applied.1.ref (“a fixed slot is set only by the act that opens its item”). Thus P1 cannot both reuse the current per-definition validator and admit the endpoint pattern the package note prescribes.\n\nChoose a compatible binding model. For example, prove a constrained mutable endpoint’s one authenticated result assignment and prohibit every other assignment, or commission an explicit native write-once-after-result form with its fold/replay rules. Do not silently widen fixed. Put the needed language/runtime work and dependencies in the staging table and slices. Add a positive native creation-result endpoint plus independent reassignment/wrong-result controls, and migrate the worked Grow declaration accordingly.\n\nF2 — P1 — Handler under is a definition name, not the manifest’s digest. Connections.from is typed Digest, while section 2 lines 135–138 requires receiver.from.under to equal that value and says the check verifies the sender’s definition at every delivery. Existing validation labels under a “definition name” (derive/src/validate/handlers.ts), and native dispatch compares it with Source.under as a name (derive/src/delivery.ts). The worked Grow handler holds under: ROOM, namely grow-room, rather than the room definition digest. Literal equality to connections.from would reject it; changing the comparison to definition.name would admit it but would not by itself prove the claimed exact pin, since two different definitions may have the same name.\n\nState the domains separately and how exact instance/pin consent is enforced: either introduce a native digest check with the required contract/runtime/replay changes, or map the static name check correctly and obtain the stronger claim through an explicit authenticated endpoint-instance check. Include two distinct digests with the same definition name as the negative control beside a passing declared peer. This is part of making the new endpoint guarantee true, not a request to rename existing names or assume name equals digest.\n\nF3 — P1 — A live item max is not a bound on retained fan-in. Section 2 lines 140–143 and the Port typing row derive a relate handler’s fan-in from the creating item type’s max. Native max bounds live items only (derive/src/handlers.ts overMax); relate copies count retained distinct owner/name/item keys, and no copy is removed (derive/src/state.ts). Consider a valid parent with max:1 and a final retired state: create child A, receive its first relation, retire the parent’s child row, then create child B. The live max remains one, but B introduces a second retained key, exceeding copies:1. The declared max comparison passes the package while the native receiver refuses type-full. No incorrect endpoint assignment is needed. The same distinction matters to a group-member-not-singleton proof: max:1 alone cannot prove one creation for the entire installation.\n\nBound lifetime distinct relation keys and instance creations, including final rows, repeated creation, multiple sends/paths and any recursion, or narrow the admitted topology to one whose lifetime bound is proved. Refuse unproved fan-in rather than present the live max as a construction guarantee. Add the retire/recreate trace and a genuinely finite passing bank. My bounded scratch probe confirms the native arithmetic: max:1 refuses a second live row, admits an opening after the old row becomes final, and the receiver still retains the old copy. It uses scripted folded state and makes no executed native-transport claim.\n\nF4 — P2 — Correct the declared-states witness. Section 4.4 permits states only over a singleton type and refuses states-not-singleton, but its positive distinction example uses the order scope’s allocation type, which is many. That declaration must fail before it can demonstrate two valid subjects sharing the state open. Use two singleton types for the passing same-state-name distinction, and retain allocation as a separately named negative control. This preserves the restriction chosen to resolve the prior ambiguous-subject finding.\n\n1. Fidelity. The revised late form resolves prior F1: bound/reason are common, absence of then means refusal, equality is fresh, branch/guard precedence and clock-behind are explicit, and S1/P2 ownership is separated. The staging table resolves the earlier P1 dependency cycle and distinguishes refusal pairs, warning witnesses and reports. The staleness paragraph now preserves behind and missing-dependency for resolved-but-uncommitted positions. The connection section attempts the stronger binding the prior F2 asked for, but F1–F3 above show that it is not yet a sound implementable rule over the native forms it cites. States has a subject, with the witness correction in F4.\n\n2. Correctness. Approval is withheld on those four findings. They concern the proposed language/check contracts, not an already delivered validatePackage implementation. The revised timed and late forms remain future work; broad wording such as “replay unchanged” does not constitute executable evidence. Order-note review 47cd6158 separately requests effective-fact/receipt and capacity rules for its new prepare/commit protocol. This package note’s reference to that draft does not approve that runtime or remove those dependencies.\n\n3. Evidence and limits. I read the full 534-line primary first, then verified the authored delta. All 2,189 regular Git leaves were independently materialized and blob-checked; the only change from authored Main32b32fc2 is this note, and all 2,188 other leaves, including source and tests, are unchanged. Whitespace passes. Base tree b7362d1e11a68a4f7585a877113f6a196d81de2a and candidate tree 346b680394075609a7af6a2fbe1551a4c2a822e0 therefore qualify for docs/testing.md’s documents-only no-repeat rule. I ran only the two focused pure native-contract probes described above, with no checkout installation, source mutation, full suite, provider or browser run.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/checker-builder-room-draft4-o_ds76x0/validation.json; /private/tmp/checker-m2a-interface-2xdpvocs/package-fixed-slot-validation.json and package-multiplicity-validation.json, with their checker-only probe sources in that scratch copy. No private woo files or external account state were read. The LF/CAL primary-source boundaries checked during draft 2 remain applicable; the current blockers follow directly from the repository’s native contracts.\n\nThis draft is authored on Main32b32fc2. Since M2a has since landed locally, an eventual landing must preserve that newer Main tree. No P-slice implementation, complete builder-room workflow, hosting or publication is accepted by this review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c7ee9a32b41bcd20ad2ce38b184470c7362dfbfb","binding":"assigned","head":"d68e4280a9c7f4e6577d754f8f0c04476eed9d89","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5d58db49033ae775ea23772b6f6327f4c730b741\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:751293b0cd462200f7822631fe9f2e227846278b\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5d58db49033ae775ea23772b6f6327f4c730b741","revie